## 1. Algebraic Foundations of Quantum Stabilizer Codes

### A. The Pauli Group & Symplectic Vector Space

Let $\mathcal{H} = (\mathbb{C}^2)^{\otimes n}$ be the Hilbert space of $n$ physical qubits. The $1$-qubit Pauli matrices are:

$$I = \begin{pmatrix} 1 & 0 \\ 0 & 1 \end{pmatrix}, \quad X = \begin{pmatrix} 0 & 1 \\ 1 & 0 \end{pmatrix}, \quad Y = \begin{pmatrix} 0 & -i \\ i & 0 \end{pmatrix}, \quad Z = \begin{pmatrix} 1 & 0 \\ 0 & -1 \end{pmatrix}$$

The $n$-qubit Pauli Group is $\mathcal{P}_n = \{\pm 1, \pm i\} \times \{I, X, Y, Z\}^{\otimes n}$. Any operator $P \in \mathcal{P}_n$ can be mapped bijectively (up to a phase) to a binary vector in the symplectic vector space $\mathbb{F}_2^{2n}$:

$$\phi: P \mapsto \mathbf{v}_P = (\mathbf{x} \mid \mathbf{z}) = (x_1, x_2, \dots, x_n \mid z_1, z_2, \dots, z_n) \in \mathbb{F}_2^{2n}$$

where:

$$P_j = X^{x_j} Z^{z_j} \implies \begin{cases}  I \mapsto (0 \mid 0) \\  X \mapsto (1 \mid 0) \\  Z \mapsto (0 \mid 1) \\  Y \mapsto (1 \mid 1)  \end{cases}$$

Two operators $P, Q \in \mathcal{P}_n$ commute if and only if their symplectic inner product vanishes:

$$\langle \mathbf{v}_P, \mathbf{v}_Q \rangle_{\text{symp}} = \mathbf{x}_P \cdot \mathbf{z}_Q + \mathbf{z}_P \cdot \mathbf{x}_Q \equiv 0 \pmod 2$$

---

### B. The Parity-Check Matrix & Syndrome Equation

A $[[n, k, d]]$ stabilizer code is defined by an isotropic subgroup $\mathcal{S} \subset \mathcal{P}_n$ generated by $m = n - k$ commuting operators $\{S_1, S_2, \dots, S_m\}$.

The code space $\mathcal{C}$ is:


$$\mathcal{C} = \left\{ \vert{}\psi\rangle \in \mathcal{H} \;\mid\; S_i \vert{}\psi\rangle = \vert{}\psi\rangle, \quad \forall i \in \{1, \dots, m\} \right\}$$

When an unobserved physical error $E \in \mathcal{P}_n$ (represented by vector $\mathbf{e} \in \mathbb{F}_2^{2n}$) corrupts state $\vert{}\psi\rangle$, measuring the stabilizer generators produces a binary **syndrome vector** $\mathbf{s} \in \mathbb{F}_2^m$:

$$H \mathbf{e} \equiv \mathbf{s} \pmod 2$$

where $H \in \mathbb{F}_2^{m \times 2n}$ is the **Quantum Parity-Check Matrix**:

$$H = \begin{pmatrix} H_X \mid H_Z \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix}  x_{1,1} & \cdots & x_{1,n} & \mid & z_{1,1} & \cdots & z_{1,n} \\ \vdots & \ddots & \vdots & \mid & \vdots & \ddots & \vdots \\ x_{m,1} & \cdots & x_{m,n} & \mid & z_{m,1} & \cdots & z_{m,n} \end{pmatrix}$$

Due to the commutativity of stabilizers, $H$ must satisfy the fundamental quantum zero-homology constraint:

$$H_X H_Z^T + H_Z H_X^T \equiv 0_{m \times m} \pmod 2$$

