## 5. Relay Belief-Propagation Mathematics (qLDPC Codes)

To decode non-planar quantum Low-Density Parity-Check (qLDPC) codes, Aether uses **Relay Belief-Propagation (Relay-BP)** over a bipartite Tanner graph $G = (V \cup C, E_{\text{Tanner}})$.

```
  Variable Nodes (V_j)                Check Nodes (C_i)
  [Physical Qubits]                   [Stabilizers]
        ( V₁ ) ─────────── m_v→c ───────────► ( C₁ )
               ◄────────── m_c→v ───────────
        ( V₂ ) ─────────────────────────────► ( C₂ )

```

### A. Message Passing Variables

* $L_j = \ln \left( \frac{P(e_j = 0)}{P(e_j = 1)} \right)$: Channel prior LLR for variable node $v_j$.
* $m_{v \to c}^{(t)}$: Message sent from variable node $v$ to check node $c$ at iteration $t$.
* $m_{c \to v}^{(t)}$: Message sent from check node $c$ to variable node $v$ at iteration $t$.

---

### B. Check-to-Variable Update (Sum-Product Rule)

Check node $c$ aggregates messages from all neighboring variable nodes $N(c) \setminus \{v\}$, incorporating syndrome measurement $s_c \in \{0, 1\}$:

$$m_{c \to v}^{(t)} = (-1)^{s_c} \cdot 2 \tanh^{-1} \left( \prod_{v' \in N(c) \setminus \{v\}} \tanh \left( \frac{m_{v' \to c}^{(t-1)}}{2} \right) \right)$$

---

### C. Variable-to-Check Update with Relay Memory Dynamics

Standard BP fails on quantum codes due to high operational degeneracy (causing symmetric trapping sets where messages oscillate indefinitely).

Relay-BP breaks these trapping sets by introducing a **disordered memory factor** $\gamma \in (0, 1)$:

$$m_{v \to c}^{(t)} = \gamma \cdot m_{v \to c}^{(t-1)} + L_v + \sum_{c' \in N(v) \setminus \{c\}} m_{c' \to v}^{(t)}$$

* $\gamma = 0 \implies$ Standard memoryless BP.
* $0 < \gamma < 1 \implies$ **Relay Memory**: Smooths message oscillations, forcing convergence out of trapping sets.

---

### D. Marginal Posterior Evaluation & Hard Decision

At iteration $t$, the total marginal LLR $\gamma_v^{(t)}$ for qubit $v$ is:

$$\gamma_v^{(t)} = L_v + \sum_{c \in N(v)} m_{c \to v}^{(t)}$$

The candidate error vector $\hat{\mathbf{e}}^{(t)} \in \mathbb{F}_2^N$ is extracted via a hard threshold decision:

$$\hat{e}_v^{(t)} = \begin{cases}  1 & \text{if } \gamma_v^{(t)} < 0 \\  0 & \text{if } \gamma_v^{(t)} \ge 0  \end{cases}$$

Decoding terminates successfully if $H \hat{\mathbf{e}}^{(t)} \equiv \mathbf{s} \pmod 2$. If BP does not converge within $t_{\text{max}}$ iterations, the soft marginal outputs $\boldsymbol{\gamma}^{(t)}$ are passed to an **Ordered Statistics Decoding (OSD)** post-processor to perform Gaussian elimination on the most reliable linearly independent columns of $H$.

